.TH TAR 1
.SH NAME
tar \- manipulate tape (or any medium) archives, in Limbo
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tar
.B {crtx}[PRTfgikmpsuvz]
[
.I archive
] [
.I file1 file2 ...
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Tar
is a structural Limbo translation of Plan 9's real
.IR tar (1)
.RB ( sys/src/cmd/tar.c ,
1588 lines in the 9front tree), not a behavioral workalike \(em see
.B doc/plan9-port.md
in the source tree for the distinction, and
.B tar.b 's
own header comment for the fuller derivation this page summarises. It
lives under
.B p9/cmd
rather than
.BR appl/cmd ;
see
.B p9/dis/README
for how to bind it in.
.PP
.I Tar
reads
.I POSIX
ustar archives (with GNU long-name and PAX extended-header support for
interop with foreign archives) and writes plain ustar archives, using
PAX extended headers of its own when a name is too long to fit the
100-byte ustar name field. Verified bidirectionally against the real
host
.B tar
(bsdtar/libarchive) during development in both directions and in both
the plain-ustar and PAX-default archive formats \(em see
.IR tartest (1)'s
own header comment for the exact commands and results, since that
check can't be run as part of the automated suite (see BUGS).
.PP
Option parsing follows the original's own
.B TARGBEGIN
convention, not the
.B ARGBEGIN
convention every other p9/cmd tool uses: only the
.I first
argument word is scanned for keyletters (so
.B "tar xvf archive file"
is the bundled form, same as any Unix tar), and the
.BR f ", " g ", and " u
keyletters each consume the
.I next whole argument word
as a value, not an attached one.
.SS Options
.TP
.B c
Create a new archive.
.TP
.B r
Append to an existing archive.
.TP
.B t
List the archive's table of contents.
.TP
.B x
Extract.
.TP
.B f\ archive
Use
.I archive
instead of standard input/output.
.TP
.B g\ gid
Numeric gid to record for created members (default 0).
.TP
.B u\ uid
Numeric uid to record for created members (default 0).
.TP
.BR i
Ignore read errors on the archive rather than stopping.
.TP
.BR k
Don't overwrite existing files on extract.
.TP
.BR m
Don't restore modification times on extract.
.TP
.BR p ", " P
Write (
.BR p ,
the default) or don't write (
.BR P )
POSIX ustar extensions.
.TP
.BR R
Don't strip a leading
.B /
or
.B #
from extracted names.
.TP
.BR s
Resync past a corrupted archive header instead of failing.
.TP
.BR T
Restore modification times, uid, and gid on extract (subject to the
BUGS note on the last two).
.TP
.BR v
Verbose: list names (create) or a permission-string/size/name line
(extract/list) as each member is processed.
.TP
.BR z
Compress/decompress with gzip. Not supported here \(em see BUGS.
.SH SOURCE
.B /p9/cmd/tar.b
(translated from
.BR sys/src/cmd/tar.c )
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR tartest (1)
.SH BUGS
Compression
.RB ( -z )
is not ported: Limbo has no fork/exec (checked directly against
.B module/sys.m ,
not assumed), so there is no way to pipe archive data through
gzip/bzip2/compress the way the original's
.BR push ()/ pushclose ()
do. On
.BR create ,
this only matters if
.B -z
is actually given (matching the original, which only invokes a
compressor when both
.B -z
and
.B -c
are set) \(em a plain
.B "tar cf foo.tar.gz ..."
with no
.B -z
still writes an uncompressed (if confusingly-named) archive here, same
as the real binary would. On
.BR extract ,
the original auto-detects compression by the archive's own filename
suffix
.RI ( .tar.gz ", " .tgz ", "
etc.)
.I unconditionally,
with no flag needed \(em so this port cannot silently match that
behaviour. Rather than read the compressed bytes as raw tar and fail
an opaque checksum error a few lines of output later, extraction on a
recognisably-compressed archive name fails immediately with a clear
message naming the suffix and the reason.
.PP
A real bug preserved from the original, found by reading the source
side by side with itself, not by testing: the header-parsing
.B parsehdr()
sets both the recorded owner name and the recorded group name from the
archive's
.I owner
name field \(em the group-name assignment should read the separate
.B gname
field one line below it in the same C struct, which the two
header-writing functions
.RB ( mkhdr (),
.IR mkpaxhdr ())
both populate correctly, making the read-side omission unambiguous.
Every group name this program has ever reported on extract or list is
silently the file's owner name instead. Preserved exactly, not
corrected, per this tree's translate-what's-there discipline \(em see
.IR file (1)'s
own
.B dict[]
bug for the precedent. Unlike that one, this was caught by reading,
not by a failing test: a real extract-then-restat check of this
specific field turned out not to be reliable in this environment
either \(em see
.IR tartest (1)'s
own BUGS.
.PP
The original's
.BR addtoar ()/ addtreetoar ()
.B chdir()
into each subdirectory during archive creation and rely on
.B getwd()
to recover the starting directory afterward. Sys has
.B chdir
but no
.BR getwd ,
so there is no general way to ask "where was I" if a nested descent
didn't fully unwind \(em this translation never chdirs at all instead,
threading full relative paths through recursion directly. Same archive
contents, different internal mechanism; the two functions are folded
into one as a result.
.PP
Block buffering is simplified: the original reads/writes several
Tblocks (a few kilobytes) per underlying I/O call for throughput, via
a shared-buffer/cursor scheme
.RB ( getblk (),
.IR curblk , " " endblk ,
.IR gothowmany ()).
This translation reads and writes exactly one 512-byte block at a
time \(em identical bytes on the wire and identical archive contents,
more syscalls, no observable format difference.
.PP
.IR longoff ()-style
seeking is not an issue here since no classifier in this file seeks
past what it has already read fully into memory \(em unlike
.IR file (1)'s
own three fd-seeking classifiers, noted on that page.
.PP
Verbose
.RB ( -v )
listing's permission-string column is a hand-written 9-character rwx
approximation, not the original's real
.B %M
.RI ( dirmodefmt )
verb \(em no setuid/sticky/special-bit rendering. Cosmetic only.
.PP
.B access(name,\ AEXIST)
has no
.B Sys
equivalent; every existence check here is
.B sys->stat()
succeeding or not, which is what the original's own call reduces to on
this filesystem model regardless.
